From 91cb7cb485673d71f3f55b588fe7e864a21ac1c3 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: daveey <daveey@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 13 Nov 2026 12:41:25 -0800
Subject: [PATCH] [daveey] delta responses: keep delta bases across Desktop
 relaunches and backend rolls

Bases were memory only on both ends. After a Desktop relaunch, an always-on backend roll,
or while the proxy splits connections between an old and a new backend, every large read
went whole again, which is when a slow link hurts most.

Measured live 2026-11-13 (hub lo capture, MacBook Desktop, t_01a1d67e18a4b1f8):
- 30 min of normal use: 2,875 eligible reads, 2,551 as deltas, 90.5% of eligible bytes
  saved. 324 whole answers carried 67.1 MB of the 69.2 MB on the wire. 269 of them named
  a base the backend no longer held: the always-on backend rolled about 6 times in 30 min,
  and during each overlap the proxy alternates old and new backends, each with its own
  store.
- First 5 min after a Desktop restart: the restart's whole first round was 48.5% of the
  window's wire bytes (6.5 MB of 13.5 MB).
- First 5 min after each roll: whole answers whose base was not held were 84-99% of the
  window's wire bytes (6-24 MB each).

Desktop (delta-reads.ts): persistDeltaBases(app) (main.ts) has the shared DeltaBodyStore
keep each read's newest copy in <userData>/delta-bodies. Each copy is written 2 s after it
arrives and synchronously on will-quit, never on the request path. One 0600 file per
(scope, path) in a 0700 dir, a header line {format, key, hash} and then the body. The
budget is 48 MB, least recently written out. A copy is read back lazily on the first use
of that read. A file is a base only when it names the same scope+path and its body hashes
to its recorded hash. Anything else is removed and the read goes whole. A failed delta
removes the file too.

Gateway (web_delta_response.py): BodyStore(disk_dir=<home>/cache/delta-bodies) is shared by
every backend of that home. Files are 0600 and named <credential namespace>-<body hash>:
two hex hashes, so the credential is never written. The budget is 64 MB, LRU by mtime. A
file is a base only when its bytes hash to its name's digest. The always-on token is
persisted and never rotated by a roll, so a rolled (or overlapping) backend can encode
against the base another one sent. A Desktop-owned `serve` mints a new token per spawn, so
its successor cannot use the old namespace: by design, nothing crosses credentials.

Nothing changes in eligibility, headers or verification. A persisted copy only ever
supplies a delta base, never an answer.
main.ts stays at its line cap: a 6-line options literal in the OAuth fetch path is now one
line.

Tests: delta-reads.test.ts (re-open gives a verified delta, debounce + quit flush,
truncated/flipped/empty/header-only file goes whole and is removed, another scope cannot
read it even renamed, 0600/0700 and no credential, failed delta removes the file, disk
budget LRU, unwritable dir stays in memory, persistDeltaBases wiring), and
test_web_delta_response.py (re-open gives a verified delta, damaged file goes whole and is
replaced, another credential namespace cannot read it, 0600/0700 and hashed names, disk
LRU budget, non-hex keys never become paths, home cache location, unwritable dir).
